WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.310
To develop a better approach
to the parameter learning problem,

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.380
we need to develop a new version
of the Viterbi algorithm.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.800
As we discussed, the Viterbi algorithm
gives a yes or no hard answer

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:21.710
to the question "Was the HMM in state k at
time i given that it emitted the string x?"

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:26.298
We now want to give a soft
answer to this approach.

00:00:26.298 --> 00:00:28.480
And to give this soft answer,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.630
we need to define the notion
of Pr(pi_i = k,x),

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:39.660
which is the unconditional
probability that the hidden path will

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:45.560
pass through state k at time i and emit x.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:51.520
For example, this question is translating
the following question for the crooked casino:

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.530
What is the probability that
the dealer was using the Fair coin

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:00.937
at the 5th flip given that
it emitted a given string?

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:08.874
So, after we define the notion
of Pr(pi_i = k,x),

00:01:08.874 --> 00:01:14.584
we notice that this is
simply the total probability

00:01:14.584 --> 00:01:20.428
of all paths passing through
the node corresponding

00:01:20.428 --> 00:01:26.572
to state k in the i'th column
of the Viterbi graph.

00:01:26.572 --> 00:01:31.943
And note that this
probability can be computed

00:01:31.943 --> 00:01:37.175
as simply path sum through
all possible paths

00:01:37.175 --> 00:01:42.551
pi such that Pr(pi_i = k,x) = 1.

00:01:42.551 --> 00:01:48.354
Also, note that the sum of
Pr(pi_i = k,x)

00:01:48.354 --> 00:01:54.007
through all possible
emitted symbols k and

00:01:54.007 --> 00:01:59.290
all possible paths x is equal to 1.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.180
We now introduce a different probability:

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:06.570
Pr(pi_i = k | x),

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:11.740
which is the conditional probability
that the HMM was in state k

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.440
at time i given that it emitted string x.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:21.000
And in the case of the crooked casino,
it corresponds to the following question:

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.924
What is the probability that
the dealer was using the fair coin

00:02:25.924 --> 00:02:31.260
at the fifth flip given that
it emitted a given string?

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:37.600
To compute the probability
that Pr(pi_i = k | x),

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.960
we notice that this probability is simply
the fraction of the product weights

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:50.740
of paths visiting node k over the weight
of all paths in the Viterbi graph.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:55.442
Or, in other words, Pr(pi_i = k | x)

00:02:55.442 --> 00:03:00.590
is simply the ratio of Pr(pi_i = k,x)

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:05.910
over Pr(x).

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:11.970
And therefore,
to find out what is Pr(pi_i = k | x),

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:17.320
we need to solve the
following soft decoding problem:

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.500
Find the probability that an HMM
was in a particular state

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.320
at a particular moment given its output.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.650
To solve the soft decoding problem,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:33.070
we first need to compute
the probability Pr(pi_i = k,x),

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:38.560
which is the total product weight of
all paths through the Viterbi graph for

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:43.830
x that pass through the highlighted
node in the graph below.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:48.683
For each such path, we can note
that it is formed by a blue subpath

00:03:48.683 --> 00:03:51.805
from the source to
the highlighted node and

00:03:51.805 --> 00:03:57.050
by the red subpath from
the highlighted node to the sink.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:01.770
And therefore, Pr(pi_i = k,x)

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:07.985
is equal to the sum of product
weights of the blue paths multiplied

00:04:07.985 --> 00:04:13.910
by the sum of the product
weights of the red paths.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:19.060
The first part of this equation,
sum of product weight of blue paths,

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:25.200
is simply the expression forward_(k,i)
that we already know how to compute.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.628
But what is the red path of this equation?

00:04:28.628 --> 00:04:33.660
Well, note that the red
path of this equation can

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:42.440
be computed if we simply will have
HMM Viterbi graph with all edges reversed.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:50.711
And we will denote the product
weight of this path as backward_(k,i).

00:04:50.711 --> 00:04:54.455
And, of course, computing backward_(k,i)

00:04:54.455 --> 00:04:58.503
will be very similar to
computing forward_(k,i).

00:04:58.503 --> 00:05:04.435
Therefore, since the reverse
edge connecting node (l,i+1)

00:05:04.435 --> 00:05:10.971
to node (k,i) in the reversed
graph has weight(k,l,i),

00:05:10.971 --> 00:05:16.298
we have a recurrence:
backward_(k,i) = sum through

00:05:16.298 --> 00:05:24.190
all states l of
backward_(l,i+1) * weight(k,l,i).

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:31.216
And therefore,
combining the forward-backward

00:05:31.216 --> 00:05:38.450
algorithm with the solution of
the Outcome Likelihood Problem,

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:43.051
we have
Pr(pi_i = k | x) = Pr(pi_i = k,x)/Pr(x),

00:05:43.051 --> 00:05:48.049
which is simply

00:05:48.049 --> 00:05:52.740
forward_(k,i) * backward_(k,i) / forward(sink).

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:57.920
We now know how to compute
each element in this formula.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:03.908
Now, after we computed
the values forward_(k,i) and

00:06:03.908 --> 00:06:07.630
backward_(k,i), let's ask
a slightly different question:

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:12.529
What is the conditional
probability that p_i = l,

00:06:12.529 --> 00:06:17.309
p_(i+1) = k given that
the emitted string is x, or

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:21.742
in other words,
what is the probability that

00:06:21.742 --> 00:06:26.550
the HMM passes through an
edge in the Viterbi graph?

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.102
And, of course, this probability is
equal to the sum of weights of all blue

00:06:33.102 --> 00:06:38.456
subpaths in this graph, multiplied
by the weight of the black edge, and

00:06:38.456 --> 00:06:42.836
multiplied by the sum of
the weights of all red subpaths.

00:06:42.836 --> 00:06:48.248
And as a result,
this expression is given by

00:06:48.248 --> 00:06:53.291
forward_(l,i) * weight(l,k,i),

00:06:53.291 --> 00:07:01.545
multiplied by the expression
backward_(k,i+1), divided by forward(sink).

